From: Taozuhong <taozuhong@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:22:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/6] MinGW: port build system and restore WTF Windows headers

Build-system port for the GTK/MinGW target:
- gio-unix-2.0 -> gio-2.0, WIN32 option defaults, mingw-compat include
  dir wiring, WTF CMakeLists adjustments.
- Restore 4 WTF Windows headers that are stripped from the release
  tarball (text/win/WCharStringExtras.h, win/SoftLinking.h,
  win/DbgHelperWin.h, win/WTFCRTDebug.h) and register them in the
  WTF_PUBLIC_HEADERS WIN32 list; WTFCRTDebug.h provides an inline no-op
  disableCRTDebugAssertDialog() under MinGW (its .cpp is not in the
  tarball while jsc.cpp calls it).
- Update 13 (WTF posix files in the MinGW GTK build): OptionsCommon.cmake
  exposes the mingw-compat include directory to every target - with
  HAVE(MMAP), <wtf/MmapSpan.h> pulls <sys/mman.h> into WebKit IPC
  translation units as well, so the WTF-local include_directories() is
  not enough - and forces HAVE_MMAP under MINGW: the CRT does not ship
  sys/mman.h, the configure-time probe would always fail (cmakeconfig.h
  had HAVE_MMAP 0, leaving MmapSpan.h empty and breaking
  OSAllocatorPOSIX.cpp); the compat shim provides the mmap/munmap/
  mprotect subset instead. WTF links ws2_32 under MinGW (UniStdExtrasUnix.cpp
  maps setNonBlock() onto ioctlsocket(FIONBIO)).
- Update 14: JavaScriptCore/CMakeLists.txt routes the Windows-ARM64
  -fno-unwind-tables (LLVM bug #47432 workaround) through the clang-cl
  "/clang:" prefix, which the MSYS2 clangarm64 GNU-frontend driver
  (clang++) rejects as an input file name ("no such file or directory:
  '/clang:-fno-unwind-tables'"); pass the flag in plain GNU form under
  MINGW. x86_64 environments never enter this branch (WTF_CPU_ARM64).
- Update 15 (two native-Win32-perl incompatibilities hit by the MSYS2
  mingw-w64 perl, $^O = MSWin32):
  WebCore/bindings/scripts/generate-bindings-all.pl spawns parallel
  generator batches via fork(), which the native Win32 perl does not
  implement ("The fork function is unimplemented", WebCoreBindings
  stamp at [2539]/8562); when fork() is unavailable the batch now runs
  serially in-process (fork semantics preserved everywhere else, the
  waitpid(-1) reaping loop stays valid).
  WebInspectorUI/Scripts/copy-user-interface-resources.pl died with
  "Please install the PEP module File::Copy::Recursive": MSYS2 does not
  ship that CPAN module for its mingw-w64 perl. The cp(1)-based fallback
  branch was restricted to $^O ne 'MSWin32'; MSYS2 environments always
  provide POSIX cp(1) on PATH, so the MSWin32 guard is dropped and the
  loop serves every non-Darwin, non-dircopy build.

  Update 18 (fixing CLANG64 JSC link failures):
  The 2.54.1 release tarball also strips the wtf/win implementation
  files, and StackTrace.h symbolizes frames through
  wtf/win/DbgHelperWin.h under OS(WINDOWS) (HAVE(BACKTRACE_SYMBOLS)
  is false on MinGW), whose single entry point
  WTF::DbgHelper::SymFromAddress was declared but defined nowhere in
  the tarball, so the JavaScriptCore link died with an undefined
  symbol. The dbghelp-backed DbgHelperWin.cpp is restored (SymInitialize
  once per process via a magic static, then SymFromAddr, accepting the
  nullptr displacement StackTrace.h passes) and registered in
  WTF_SOURCES. CurrentTime.cpp raises the timer resolution through
  winmm's timeBeginPeriod()/timeEndPeriod(), and WTF is a static
  library in this port, so winmm and dbghelp are appended to
  WTF_LIBRARIES and propagate to the JavaScriptCore (and later
  WebKit) links.

  Update 19 (fixing the round-18 link-library plumbing):
  The dbghelp/winmm entries appended to WTF_LIBRARIES inside the WIN32
  source-registration block were silently discarded: the tarball's own
  set(WTF_LIBRARIES ${CMAKE_DL_LIBS} ICU::data ICU::i18n ICU::uc)
  further down the same file resets the variable after that block, and
  only appends made after the reset survive into the
  target_link_libraries(WTF PUBLIC ${WTF_LIBRARIES}) that
  _WEBKIT_TARGET_SETUP(WTF) runs when WEBKIT_FRAMEWORK(WTF) finalizes
  the target. The two libraries therefore never reached the
  JavaScriptCore link line, and ld.lld kept reporting "undefined
  symbol: __declspec(dllimport) timeBeginPeriod / timeEndPeriod /
  SymFromAddr / SymInitialize" - the four remaining round-18 link
  errors, all four import-library plumbing. The ws2_32 entry already
  living after the reset is the proof the position works (its symbols
  resolved on the same link line). The entries now move into the
  existing `if (MINGW)` block right next to ws2_32; the round-18 WTF
  sources registration (win/DbgHelperWin.cpp) is unaffected.

  Update 21 (fixing the round-21 UCRT64 .gmo failures):
  Source/WebCore/platform/gtk/po/CMakeLists.txt replaces msgmerge with
  the POSIX no-op ":" - upstream deliberately avoids rewriting the .po
  files. On Windows CMake drives custom commands through cmd.exe /C,
  and cmd.exe has no ":" command, so every one of the 55 translation
  targets died with "':' is not recognized as an internal or external
  command" (first hits po/ar.gmo, po/as.gmo at [6985..6987]/8553; the
  msgfmt half of the chain, found in /usr/bin, was never reached). The
  substitution now becomes "${CMAKE_COMMAND} -E true" under WIN32:
  CMake's portable no-op ignores the msgmerge arguments and exits 0
  (verified against cmake 3.31), keeping the no-msgmerge semantics
  byte-identical; non-Windows keeps ":". Ninja aborting on these
  failures also left native Win32 perl.exe children running orphaned,
  which surfaced downstream as a "build hangs around perl" report -
  with the .gmo targets building, ninja no longer aborts mid-flight.

  Update 25 (fixing the round-25 CLANGARM64 WebKit PCH failures and
  pre-empting the Windows DLL link failure): two PlatformGTK.cmake
  changes.

  - Upstream defines DATADIR globally for every WebKit-directory target.
    MinGW's ole2.h contains "typedef enum tagDATADIR { ... } DATADIR;"
    and windows.h (dragging in ole2.h -> objbase.h -> objidl.h) is
    reached through the WebKit prefix headers, so the macro rewrites the
    enum typedef name into a string literal, "} \"/clangarm64/share\";",
    i.e. "expected ';' after enum" + "expected unqualified-id" at
    objidl.h:10669 - CI hit it in all four WebKit PCH targets (WebKit,
    WebKitUIProcess, WebKitWebProcess, WebKitGPUProcess). The define is
    now skipped under WIN32: the only in-tree consumer,
    BubblewrapLauncher.cpp, is wholly wrapped in
    ENABLE(BUBBLEWRAP_SANDBOX) (off in this build) and is never compiled
    on Windows, and no mingw-w64 header references LOCALEDIR/LIBDIR/
    PKGLIBDIR/PKGLIBEXECDIR (verified), which stay defined. Sandbox: the
    pristine TU reproduces the CI errors line-for-line on the clang64
    and clangarm64 header sets, and objidl.h parses clean with the
    remaining defines on both.

  - Nothing in upstream's CMake attaches the GTK include directories to
    the WebKit target (only MiniBrowser/TestWebKitAPI link GTK::GTK);
    on ELF that works because shared libraries may leave symbols
    undefined and applications link GTK themselves. Both halves break on
    Windows: the WebKit PCH includes UIProcess/ViewSnapshotStore.h (via
    SessionState.h, reached from generated IPC message headers) whose
    GTK4 branch has "#include <gtk/gtk.h>" -> "fatal error: 'gtk/gtk.h'
    file not found" in the WebKitWebProcess PCH, and PE DLLs must
    resolve every referenced symbol at link time (WebKitWebViewBase and
    friends use GtkWidget/GdkTexture directly). WebKit_LIBRARIES now
    gains GTK::GTK under WIN32, providing both the include directories
    and the gtk-4/gdk-4 import libraries; the PCH subtargets inherit it
    because WEBKIT_DEFINE_SUBTARGET copies the parent's LINK_LIBRARIES
    property into every WebKit{UIProcess,WebProcess,GPUProcess,NetworkProcess}
    prefix target.

  Update 26 (fixing the round-26 CLANG64 jsc link failure): WTFString.h
  declares String::wideCharacters() under OS(WINDOWS) with
  WTF_EXPORT_PRIVATE, and two in-tree Windows call sites consume it:
  jsc.cpp's fetchModuleFromLocalFileSystem() (\\?\ long-path _wstat/
  _wfopen module loading) and WebCore's LocalizedStrings.cpp, whose
  "#elif PLATFORM(WIN)" image-title branch fires before USE(GLIB) on
  Windows GTK builds. The definition lives in
  Source/WTF/wtf/text/win/StringWin.cpp, an upstream file the GTK
  release tarball strips together with the whole wtf/text/win/
  directory (same stripping class as the four WTF Windows headers
  restored above; PlatformJSCOnly.cmake, which owns upstream's WIN32
  WTF source list, is not included by GTK builds). Restore the file
  verbatim from the webkitgtk-2.54.1 tag and register it in
  Source/WTF/wtf/PlatformGTK.cmake under if (WIN32) so the WTF static
  library carries the exported symbol for every final PE link: CI died
  with "undefined symbol: __declspec(dllimport)
  WTF::String::wideCharacters() const" while linking bin/jsc.exe at
  [8117]/8563 on CLANG64 (WebCore's copy would have hit the same
  missing import for WebKit.dll via LocalizedStrings.cpp).


  Update 28 (defusing the round-28 UCRT64 CI hang): after the Skia static
  archive link starts, the only schedulable edges left in the ninja graph
  are the WebKit-forwarding-headers custom target (everything downstream
  consumes the ForwardingHeaders output) and that link itself, and the CI
  log stops at the perl command for 5h40m until the 6h job timeout cancels
  the run - no other edge ever gets scheduled. generate-forwarding-
  headers.pl itself is bounded (fixed directory walk over Source/WebKit,
  no stdin, no fork), so the stall is process-level in the native Win32
  perl (MINGW_PACKAGE_PREFIX-perl, $^O=MSWin32) under the CI environment;
  the identical step completes on the CLANG64 runner. The custom target
  now takes its interpreter from the new WEBKIT_FORWARDING_HEADERS_PERL
  cache variable (defaults to PERL_EXECUTABLE, so non-MinGW behavior is
  unchanged), letting the packager point this single step at the MSYS2
  POSIX perl (PKGBUILD passes
  -DWEBKIT_FORWARDING_HEADERS_PERL=$(cygpath -w /usr/bin/perl.exe)) while
  every other perl-based generator keeps the native interpreter that the
  first ~7000 build steps already ran successfully.

  Update 29 (defusing the round-29 UCRT64 CI hang for good): the Update 28
  interpreter override never took effect on the runner - the ninja command
  still shows D:\M\msys64\ucrt64\bin\perl.exe - so the flag delivery itself
  is unreliable (most plausibly /usr/bin/perl.exe is absent on the CI
  image, the PKGBUILD guard skipped the -D silently, and the native perl
  step hung a second time at [7041]/8554). The custom target now also
  understands WEBKIT_FORWARDING_HEADERS_SKIP: when the packager sets it,
  the perl step is replaced by a "cmake -E echo" no-op and the headers are
  pre-generated out-of-band instead - the PKGBUILD runs
  generate-forwarding-headers.pl directly under the MSYS2 POSIX perl from
  bash before "cmake --build". The script is deterministic and its output
  layout (<build>/DerivedSources/ForwardingHeaders/WebKit/<basename>,
  content "#include \"WebKit/<relative path>\"") is independent of the
  interpreter's path syntax, so the ninja graph consumes byte-identical
  files. WEBKIT_FORWARDING_HEADERS_PERL stays as the fallback for manual
  builds that skip the pre-generation.
  Update 30 (pre-flight hardening for the post-compile link and install
  stages, which no CI round has reached yet on this package): two defects
  that would fire after every compile edge passes, both reproduced in the
  sandbox with the same toolchain families and both documented behavior,
  fixed before they can burn a full CI cycle.
  (a) PlatformGTK.cmake added "-Wl,--version-script,webkitglib-symbols.map"
  to the WebKit link flags unconditionally for !DEVELOPER_MODE, but that is
  ELF-only machinery. MSYS2's LLVM environments link exclusively with LLD
  ("Only uses LLVM tools, LLD as a linker", msys2.org environments doc), and
  lld's MinGW driver rejects the option outright: "lld: error: unknown
  argument: --version-script" (reproduced with lld 19 via the clang MinGW
  driver), which would have killed the libwebkitgtk-6.0.dll link on
  CLANG64/CLANGARM64. GNU ld (UCRT64) accepts and silently ignores it on PE
  and PE has no version sections anyway: the DLL's export surface on Windows
  comes from WEBKIT_API and WK_EXPORT expanding to __declspec(dllexport)
  under BUILDING_WebKit (WebKitDefines.h.in, WKDeclarationSpecifiers.h),
  which the sandbox confirmed by inspecting the produced export table. The
  version script (and its LINK_DEPENDS entry) now sits behind NOT WIN32, so
  ELF builds are byte-for-byte unchanged and the flag vanishes from every
  PE link line. The WEBKIT_API/WK_EXPORT dllexport path also covers the
  WebProcessMain/NetworkProcessMain/GPUProcessMain entry points the
  symbols.map lists for ELF, so nothing else is needed for PE.
  (b) The install rules are ELF-shaped. install(TARGETS WebKit WebProcess
  NetworkProcess RUNTIME DESTINATION libexec) names only a RUNTIME
  destination: on DLL platforms that sends libwebkitgtk-6.0.dll to libexec
  and - per the CMake install() docs ("If either a RUNTIME or ARCHIVE
  destination is specified ... the other component is not installed",
  verified in a sandbox CMAKE_SYSTEM_NAME=Windows configure+install) -
  silently drops the import library, leaving -lwebkitgtk-6.0 from
  webkitgtk-6.0.pc dangling and the DLL outside every DLL search path.
  WIN32 branches now split the group: the WebKit DLL goes to bin (MSYS2
  requires runtime DLLs on PATH; the libexec-resident process executables
  import it from there) with its import library in lib beside the .pc
  files, and the WebProcess/NetworkProcess executables keep libexec as
  their RUNTIME destination (GPUProcess's separate rule is unaffected - an
  executable has only a RUNTIME artifact). The same single-DESTINATION
  form for the SHARED JavaScriptCore target lands
  libjavascriptcoregtk-6.0.dll in lib, where no DLL search ever looks, so
  it gets the same WIN32 split (DLL -> bin, import library -> lib).
  The injected-bundle MODULE (no import library) and the process
  executables need no changes; ELF destinations are preserved verbatim in
  the else() branches.

  Update 31 (three fixes for the round-31 CI cycle):
  (a) PlatformGTK.cmake: WEBKIT_FORWARDING_HEADERS_SKIP now defaults to ON
  on Windows. The round-29 pre-generation bypass ships both halves of the
  workaround (out-of-band perl run + a -DWEBKIT_FORWARDING_HEADERS_SKIP=ON
  flag), but the round-30 CI run shows the runner still executing the
  in-build step with the native ucrt64 perl: the PKGBUILD used to pass
  SKIP=ON only after a successful pre-generation, and whenever its
  /usr/bin/perl.exe probe failed it silently fell back to the stalling
  path. A lost flag must never re-arm a known-deadlocking edge, so the
  default flips on WIN32 (non-Windows hosts keep the in-build step); the
  explicit -D from the packager still wins, and the PKGBUILD now aborts
  immediately with a clear diagnostic when the POSIX perl is missing
  instead of silently re-enabling the step that hung the UCRT64 runner for
  6 hours in three consecutive rounds.
  (b) Source/WTF/wtf/glib/GSpanExtras.h: the CLANG64 cycle reached
  WebKitWebContext.cpp with 7 warnings + 6 errors; the warnings are all
  -Winconsistent-dllimport on g_malloc()/g_malloc0()/g_realloc()/
  g_try_malloc()/g_try_malloc0()/g_try_realloc()/g_strfreev(). The header
  re-declared them inside a hand-rolled extern "C" block without the
  GLIB_AVAILABLE_IN_ALL (dllimport on Windows) attribute, while
  GUniquePtr.h - included a few lines above - already pulls the full
  <glib.h> prototypes through <gio/gio.h>. A redeclaration without the
  attribute makes clang drop the previous dllimport ("previous 'dllimport'
  ignored"), which downgrades every one of those calls to a non-import
  call shape. The GLib headers own these prototypes; the block is removed.
  (c) Source/WTF/wtf/glib/GRefPtr.h: same disease one include up. The
  extern "C" block redeclaring g_object_unref()/g_object_ref_sink()/
  g_dbus_node_info_ref()/g_dbus_node_info_unref() (plus the opaque
  GDBus typedefs) collides with <glib-object.h>/<gio/gio.h> in both
  include orders: first-dllimport-then-bare trips
  -Winconsistent-dllimport, first-bare-then-dllimport trips
  -Wdll-attribute-on-redeclaration (reproduced both ways in the sandbox
  against the real MSYS2 glib 2.90 headers). Both headers are included
  above the removed block, which owns the types and all four prototypes.
