From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Taozuhong <taozuhong@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 07:22:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/6] MinGW: WTF threading, compiler compatibility and portable

- winpthreads threading model, SuspendThread suspension,
  GetProcessTimes CPU time, sys/mman.h compat shim (mmap family),
  Win32Handle, UnixFileDescriptor close semantics.
- Threading.h s_key declaration guard relaxed to
  !HAVE(FAST_TLS) && (!OS(WINDOWS) || USE(PTHREADS)) (the GLib port
  uses winpthreads on Windows, see PlatformUse.h); align
  signalHandlerSuspendResume declaration (&& !OS(WINDOWS), mingw-w64
  has no siginfo_t); Threading.cpp storage guard likewise;
  ThreadingPOSIX.cpp uses std::make_unique for PlatformRegisters
  (the WinAPI CONTEXT has no TZoneMalloc tagging).
- FunctionTraits.h: declare the sysv_abi specializations through the
  SysVFunction type alias - GCC drops attributes written between the
  return type and (*) inside template partial-specialization
  arguments, causing a redefinition conflict; the alias keeps them
  on both compilers.
- CurrentTime.cpp: replace the MSVC-only integer literal suffix
  0x100000000I64 with the portable 0x100000000LL (long long and
  __int64 are the same 64-bit signed type on Windows; MSVC 2013+
  accepts LL as well). Whole-tree scan confirms it is the only
  MSVC-style literal suffix.
- Update 13 (fixing the CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WTF build, [2386..2390]/8562;
  applies to ucrt64 alike since the gaps are CRT-level, not compiler-level):
  FileHandle.h/FileSystem.h keep the int CRT-descriptor flavour for the GLib
  port even on Windows - PlatformGTK.cmake compiles the posix/* and glib/*
  implementations, so the OS(WINDOWS) HANDLE/FILE_ID_128 flavour of
  PlatformFileHandle/PlatformFileID cannot work there (fstat(void*) and 12
  more errors in FileHandlePOSIX.cpp); the Win32 flavour stays reserved for
  ports driving files through the Windows API. FileHandlePOSIX.cpp emulates
  flock(2) via LockFileEx (LOCK_SH/EX/NB/UN match the FileLockMode encoding,
  static_asserts intact) and maps fsync() onto _commit(). FileSystemPOSIX.cpp
  replaces the missing sys/statvfs.h with GetDiskFreeSpaceA for
  volumeFileBlockSize(), uses _O_NOINHERIT instead of the missing O_CLOEXEC
  and mkstemp() instead of the missing mkostemp(). TextBreakIteratorInternalICUUnix.cpp
  falls back to LC_CTYPE when the UCRT lacks LC_MESSAGES. UniStdExtrasUnix.cpp
  maps the fcntl(2) CLOEXEC/O_NONBLOCK requests onto SetHandleInformation()
  and ioctlsocket(FIONBIO) (WTF gains ws2_32, see 0001). The sys/mman.h shim
  gains MAP_FILE (FileHandle::map() passes MAP_FILE | MAP_SHARED).
- Update 14 (fixing 'memoryStatus' was not declared in
  generic/MemoryFootprintGeneric.cpp on all three environments):
  PlatformGTK.cmake falls into the generic memory-footprint branch on
  Windows, but the MemoryStatus API in AvailableMemory.h/.cpp is guarded
  to PLATFORM(IOS_FAMILY) || OS(LINUX) || OS(FREEBSD). Extend both
  guards with OS(WINDOWS) (the file already carries an OS(WINDOWS)
  branch for availableMemory() upstream) and implement memoryStatus()
  on top of GetProcessMemoryInfo(): the working set size is the
  per-process analog of the Linux cgroup footprint / BSD RSS values;
  GetProcessMemoryInfo resolves to K32GetProcessMemoryInfo in
  kernel32.dll on mingw-w64 (link probe: no psapi import library
  needed). generic/MemoryFootprintGeneric.cpp then compiles unchanged.

- Update 16 (two clang/MinGW failures in the JSC assembler area):
  MacroAssemblerX86_64.cpp expands ASM_PREVIOUS_SECTION to ".previous"
  for every compiler except MSVC, but LLVM's integrated assembler does
  not implement .previous for PE/COFF targets, so both
  DEFINE_PROBE_TRAMPOLINE blocks (ctiMasmProbeTrampoline and
  ctiMasmProbeTrampolineAVX) die with "unknown directive" at
  <inline asm>:128/:256 on the mingw clang builds. Every block already
  begins with an explicit ".text" directive, so the trailing section
  restore is unnecessary there: the macro now also expands to nothing
  for OS(WINDOWS), and ".previous" is kept for ELF targets.
  PerfLog.cpp wraps _open/_fdopen as global open/fdopen shims for the
  MSVC CRT under OS(WINDOWS); the MinGW-w64 CRT already declares plain
  fdopen in <stdio.h> (and open in <io.h>), so the wrappers conflict:
  "static declaration of 'fdopen' follows non-static declaration" at
  PerfLog.cpp:60. The wrappers are unused in the rest of the file and
  only needed when COMPILER(MSVC); the block is now guarded accordingly.

  Update 17 (fixing one CLANG64 and one CLANGARM64 JSC failure):
  ProfilerSupport.cpp carries the same MSVC CRT wrapper block as
  PerfLog.cpp (round 16): open/_open and fdopen/_fdopen shims under
  OS(WINDOWS). The MinGW-w64 CRT already declares fdopen in <stdio.h>
  (stdio.h:875 in the MSYS2 clang64 headers), so clang rejects the
  redefinition with "static declaration of 'fdopen' follows non-static
  declaration" at ProfilerSupport.cpp:54. The wrappers are unused in
  the rest of the file and only exist for COMPILER(MSVC); the block is
  guarded accordingly, mirroring the PerfLog.cpp fix.
  LowLevelInterpreter.cpp defines OFFLINE_ASM_ALIGN_TRAP(align) by
  token-pasting (OFFLINE_ASM_ALIGN_TRAP_##align) in the
  CPU(ARM64) && OS(WINDOWS) branch, but only provided the 256 and 64
  entries. The offlineasm emits alignment 512 for the Wasm ipint
  atomic handlers (alignAtomicIPInt is 2 * alignIPInt, i.e. 512 with
  the default alignIPInt of 256; 79 labels in the generated
  LLIntAssembly.h, first hit at ipint_memory_atomic_notify_atomic_validate),
  and 1024 when LLINT_TRACING is on. The undefined OFFLINE_ASM_ALIGN_TRAP_512
  token survives inside the __asm__ string block and dies with
  "expected ')'" at LLIntAssembly.h:91182. Following the upstream COFF
  convention of that branch (.align N = 2^N bytes), the missing
  entries are provided as .align 9 / .align 10.

  Update 18 (fixing CLANG64 JSC link failures and a latent UCRT64 one):
  interpreter/CallFrame.h expands _AddressOfReturnAddress() inside the
  OS(WINDOWS) && CPU(X86_64) DECLARE_CALL_FRAME macro, but MinGW
  <intrin.h> only declares that MSVC intrinsic (via __MACHINEI) without
  ever providing a definition, so every JIT operation referencing it
  died at the JavaScriptCore link with "undefined symbol:
  _AddressOfReturnAddress" (~658 references from the DFG unified
  sources alone). MinGW compilers now reach the saved caller frame
  pointer without the intrinsic, and the rbp asm clobber is dropped
  because both toolchains reject the original combination: GCC refuses
  to clobber rbp in functions whose frame chain it relies on ("bp
  cannot be used in 'asm' here", e.g. operationAtomicsNotify), and
  clang no longer needs it because the builtin establishes the frame.
  GCC uses __builtin_frame_address(1), which walks the frame chain
  exactly like on other platforms (verified to emit
  "push %rbp; mov %rsp,%rbp; mov 0x0(%rbp),%rax", i.e. the same slot
  the intrinsic path reads). MinGW clang keeps the Windows-specific
  __builtin_frame_address(1) == __builtin_frame_address(0) quirk the
  upstream comment describes, so it instead dereferences
  __builtin_frame_address(0), which forces a proper frame pointer and
  reads the caller frame pointer saved by the prologue - the same
  value the intrinsic path computes. JIT operations carry
  __attribute__((sysv_abi)) on Windows, so GCC/clang cannot inline
  them and the frame walk always stays at the operation's own frame.
  threads/Signals.cpp is wrapped in #if OS(UNIX), yet WTFConfig.cpp
  calls SignalHandlers::initialize()/finalize() unconditionally and
  the Wasm fault-signaling path calls addSignalHandler() and
  activateSignalHandlersFor(), so the CLANG64 JSC link failed on all
  four symbols. A Windows backend is restored under #elif OS(WINDOWS),
  mirroring the stripped wtf/win implementation: handlers are
  registered in the g_wtfConfig.signalHandlers arrays and dispatched
  from a first-chance vectored exception handler installed once in
  SignalHandlers::finalize(); EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION and
  EXCEPTION_IN_PAGE_ERROR map to Signal::AccessFault with the
  ExceptionInformation[1] faulting address, and a Handled result
  resumes execution with the (possibly modified) CONTEXT, which is
  what the Wasm fault-based bounds checking requires.
  MemoryPressureHandler's constructor registers
  windowsMeasurementTimerFired() for the Windows measurement timer,
  but the stripped wtf/win/MemoryPressureHandlerWin.cpp was its only
  upstream definition; a conservative implementation is restored in
  MemoryPressureHandler.cpp (respond to memory pressure unless the
  policy is still Unrestricted; the timer is only armed by explicit
  callers).

  Update 21 (fixing the round-21 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WebCore failures):
  Upstream MathExtras.h works around the UCRT atan2(inf, inf) = NaN
  bug by defining a function-like macro
  "#define atan2(x, y) wtf_atan2(x, y)" for all of OS(WINDOWS) and
  never undefines it. The macro stays active when a translation unit
  later includes standard headers that spell std::atan2 inside their
  own templates - first seen with libc++'s <complex> pulled in through
  platform/audio/Biquad.h by JSDOMWindow.cpp ([7467]/8563): the tokens
  inside those templates became "std::wtf_atan2" and every
  instantiation died with "error: no member named 'wtf_atan2' in
  namespace 'std'". <cmath> is already included before the macro;
  <complex> now joins it, so libc++ and libstdc++ parse their atan2
  call sites before the macro exists and all later inclusions hit
  their include guards. WebKit's own unqualified atan2() calls keep
  working (verified in the preprocessor: "atan2(y, x)" still expands
  to "wtf_atan2(y, x)"), so the infinity handling is preserved at
  every other call site.

  Update 22 (fixing the round-22 CLANG64/CLANGARM64 WebCore failures):
  The Windows branches added in Update 16 carried Win32 handles through
  UnixFileDescriptor's int payload with static_cast, and
  static_cast<int>(static_cast<intptr_t>(duplicate)) at the Duplicate
  constructor is ill-formed C++ (a pointer-to-integer conversion needs
  reinterpret_cast), so both clang environments died inside
  UnifiedSource-platform-4.cpp with "static_cast from 'HANDLE' (aka
  'void *') to 'intptr_t' is not allowed" ([7539..7543]/8563). The
  packing now lives in two small helpers, handleFromIntValue() and
  intFromHandleValue(): they truncate a handle to its lower 32 bits
  when packing and zero-extend (never sign-extend) on the way back out,
  per the Windows 32/64-bit handle interop guarantee - the previous
  sign-extending reconstruction would have corrupted handles with bit
  31 set. The Duplicate constructor and the destructor both use the
  helpers now.
